{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cff9xvMxTKvia4axrTAzi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I rewrote the candidate so it should fit within the 30-minute limit, and the self-check prints **OK**. It hasn't been through admission's full fit yet, so the speed and convergence gates are still open.\n\n**Hypothesis (`switching_then_balance_semiorder`):** People compare the two sequences in order. First they ask which one switches between H and T at a rate closer to their own ideal. Only when the two switch about equally often do they fall back on a second test: they pick the sequence with the more even heads/tails count. So a sequence's balance matters next to a partner with the same number of switches and hardly matters next to one that switches clearly more or less. This targets the critique's top finding: at equal switch counts, people choose the more balanced sequence more often (0.677 observed vs 0.527 predicted).\n\n**Why the last attempt was too slow:** the switching difference decided how much weight balance got, through two free parameters (`kappa`, `tau`) that traded off against each other. Each person also had their own sensitivity, which added a second set of 400 latent values.\n\n**What I changed:**\n- **Gate is now fixed:** the weight on balance comes straight from the stimulus. It is 1 when both sequences have the same switch count and halves with each switch of difference. It is computed once per pair in `compute_features`, not in the model graph.\n- **Fewer parameters:** I removed `tau` and the per-person sensitivities. What remains:\n  - a personal ideal switching rate for each person, from a population distribution (400 slots, so the design can still predict new participants);\n  - one shared sensitivity that scales with sequence length;\n  - one balance weight (`kappa`);\n  - a left-button bias.\n- The likelihood is fully vectorised over trials.\n\n**Check results:**\n- The smoke fit (100 tune + 100 draws, one chain) took 89 s and gave a finite ELPD-LOO of −2430.7.\n- **Run time is my rough estimate:** scaling the smoke fit up, admission's full fit should take about 15 minutes per sampling run. That is under the limit but not by a wide margin.\n- **Convergence is unchecked:** a single chain can't report R-hat.\n- **LOO estimate is shaky:** 4.1% of trials have Pareto k above 0.5, against a 1% tolerance. That doesn't block admission, but it marks the model's ELPD-LOO as unreliable, so it can't be exported as the winner and the incumbent comparison may be inaccurate.\n\nAll three files (`hypothesis.md`, `model_name.txt`, `candidate.py`) are written to `candidate_2_repair_1/`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2951,"cache_read_input_tokens":62893,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2951,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5dd6c6fa-3ad6-4acd-b609-c52173a6c69a","uuid":"b45198c9-c8d9-440c-ac08-9b4d59dccec4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T10:35:43.848Z","request_id":"req_011Cff9xu73fSDbqebogVCLd"}
